#{[17592186555965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555978 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555965 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "216d3edd-79b6-5b78-9d7a-c69bb1f635e7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555965 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (1)+(-2)\\cdot (2 t)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(-3)+(- 4 t)+(- 6 t) \\\\  & =- (10 t + 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555961 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555975 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x - 3 y - 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t)\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ed8bb8a-ea3c-5edb-81c1-5b691ab3509b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555975 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(-2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1)+(- 6 t)+(-2) \\\\  & =- (6 t + 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555983 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555990 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right)$ and $r(t)={(t + 1)}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\,j+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f25a065-b9d2-5631-91bf-0e1b3f75fc51" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555990 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,k \\\\  & =(4\\left(-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={(t + 1)}^{2},y={e}^{(t + 1)},$ and $z={e}^{(t + 1)}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(4\\left(-{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({(t + 1)}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k \\\\  & =(2(t + 1))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((4\\left(-{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,k)\\cdot ((2(t + 1))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\left(-2(t + 1){e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t + 1)}+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t + 1)}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(-2(t + 1){e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})+2{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(2{e}^{2(t + 1)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})-(t + 1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2}))\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555994 #edb/r