\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)}+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)} \\\\  & =-2(t - 2){e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})+2{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2(t - 2)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})-(t - 2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555999 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555974 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555999 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555999 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=2 x - 3 y + 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555999 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b831457d-f088-5348-bc3d-27f5556d6376" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186555999 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(2)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(2)+(- 6 t)+(4 t) \\\\  & =2 \\left(1 - t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 